---

<Base
  lang="en"
  title="Run Claude Code in a native Mac app | Grove for Mac"
  description="Run Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI and other AI coding agents in a native Mac app, with a real shell, diff review and git worktrees. $9.99 once."
  path="/claude-code-mac-app.html"
  navVariant="page"
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>

<!-- GUIDE CONTENT -->
<section class="section legal guide">
  <div class="section-inner legal-inner">

    <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="legal-title">Run Claude Code in a native Mac app</h1>
    <p class="legal-updated">Grove for Mac · Updated November 2026</p>

    <div class="legal-callout">
      <strong>Short version:</strong> Grove gives Claude Code, and other CLI coding agents, a home in a native
      macOS window. The agent runs exactly as it does in Terminal, next to a real shell, your file tree and a
      GitHub-style diff of what it changed. All of it follows the git worktree you're working in.
    </div>

    <div class="legal-prose">

      <h2>Claude Code, unchanged</h2>
      <p>
        Claude Code is a terminal program, and Grove keeps it that way. It runs the real <code>claude</code>
        command in a real terminal (a PTY), in your project folder, with your own environment, configuration and
        login. Grove doesn't proxy your requests, add its own AI model, inject credentials or pass your code to
        the agent as arguments. You get the same Claude Code, with a better workspace around it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Grove finds agents installed in the usual places, including <code>~/.local/bin</code>, Homebrew and Bun,
        even when you open the app from the Dock rather than a terminal.
      </p>

      <h2>Any agent, chosen per project</h2>
      <p>
        Grove isn't tied to one vendor. It runs Claude Code, opencode, Codex, Gemini CLI and Aider. Each checkout
        remembers which agent you picked, and you can open several agent tabs side by side. Your tabs, their
        order and each tab's agent come back after a relaunch.
      </p>

      <h2>Review what the agent changed</h2>
      <p>
        The Changes tab lists every modified file with a GitHub-style diff and syntax highlighting. Files you've
        looked at are marked as viewed, and the mark clears if the agent changes them again. Click "+" on any line
        to leave a comment. Comments collect into a single review, and <strong>Send</strong> types the whole
        review into the agent's prompt so you can read it before pressing Return. Grove never submits it for you.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <img src="/screenshots/changes-review.png" width="1800" height="1125" loading="lazy"
             alt="Grove's Changes tab with a unified diff, an inline comment box and a Send to Agent button" />
        <figcaption>Inline diff comments are batched into one review, then typed into Claude Code's prompt.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Prompts and commands you reuse</h2>
      <p>
        Save the prompts you keep retyping, and the shell commands you run every day, per project or in a shared
        library. Run them from the toolbar or the ⌘K palette. Templates fill in values like
        <code>&#123;&#123;branch&#125;&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;&#123;date&#125;&#125;</code> or your clipboard, and ask for anything else before running.
      </p>

      <h2>Parallel work with git worktrees</h2>
      <p>
        Give each agent its own branch in its own worktree. Switching worktrees in the sidebar moves the agent,
        shell, files, diff and git panel together, and each agent keeps running while you're looking at another
        one. See <a href="/git-worktree-app-mac.html">A git worktree app for Mac</a> for how that works.
      </p>

      <h2>Built for the Mac</h2>
      <p>
        Grove is written in Swift and SwiftUI, not Electron. It follows your system's appearance, launches
        quickly, and is signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple. Closing the window doesn't stop
        anything: your agents and shells keep running until you quit.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <h3>Do I need a Claude subscription?</h3>
      <p>
        Grove doesn't include an AI model. You need Claude Code installed and your own Anthropic account, the
        same as in Terminal. What the agent sends is between you and its provider; Grove itself doesn't upload
        your repository.
      </p>
      <h3>Can I use it without Claude Code?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Use opencode, Codex, Gemini CLI or Aider instead, or just the shell, files and diff panes.</p>
      <h3>What does it cost?</h3>
      <p>
        There's a 7-day free trial. After that, a license is $9.99 one-time for one Mac, with every future update
        included. Grove needs macOS 26 or later.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="legal-callout guide-cta">
      <strong>Try Grove for Mac free for 7 days.</strong> Signed and notarized by Apple, no subscription.
      <p><a href="/#download" role="button" class="btn btn-primary">Download Grove</a></p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

</Base>
